# @onlyoffice/docspace-webhooks-sdk

The ONLYOFFICE DocSpace Webhooks SDK for TypeScript is a library that provides tools for receiving webhook deliveries from DocSpace. It verifies the signature on each delivery and decodes its payload into ready-to-use models.

It is the inbound counterpart to `@onlyoffice/docspace-api-sdk`: that library calls DocSpace, this one handles what DocSpace sends you. It makes no requests of its own.

For more information, please visit [https://helpdesk.onlyoffice.com/hc/en-us](https://helpdesk.onlyoffice.com/hc/en-us)

## Requirements

Node.js 18+

## Installation

```
npm install @onlyoffice/docspace-webhooks-sdk
```

## Usage

A receiver does two things, in this order: verify, then parse.

```ts
if (!verifySignature(body, secret, signature)) {
    return res.status(401).end();
}

const hook = parseWebhook(body);
```

`body` must be the **raw bytes exactly as received**. A body that has been through `JSON.parse` and `JSON.stringify` is a different byte sequence and will never match, so mount `express.raw()` on the webhook route rather than `express.json()`.

Answer as soon as the signature checks out and do the real work afterwards. A delivery gets five attempts over roughly 31 seconds, after which it is abandoned; a subscription that goes three days without a single successful delivery is switched off.

## Getting Started

```ts
import express from 'express';
import {
    verifySignature,
    parseWebhook,
    isKnownWebhook,
    readSignatureHeader,
} from '@onlyoffice/docspace-webhooks-sdk';

const app = express();
const secret = 'YOUR_SUBSCRIPTION_SECRET_KEY';

// express.raw() keeps the body as a Buffer; express.json() would reparse it and
// the signature would never match.
app.post('/webhook', express.raw({ type: 'application/json' }), (req, res) => {
    const signature = readSignatureHeader(req.headers);

    if (!verifySignature(req.body, secret, signature)) {
        return res.status(401).end();
    }

    const hook = parseWebhook(req.body);

    // Acknowledge now; anything slow belongs after this point.
    res.status(200).end();

    if (!isKnownWebhook(hook)) {
        return;               // an event added after this build
    }

    switch (hook.trigger) {
        case 'user.created':
        case 'user.updated':
            console.log(`${hook.trigger}: ${hook.payload.userName}`);
            break;

        case 'file.created':
        case 'folder.created':
            // Files and folders share one payload shape; fileEntryType tells
            // them apart: 1 folder, 2 file.
            console.log(
                `${hook.trigger}: ` +
                `${hook.payload.fileEntryType === 2 ? 'file' : 'folder'} ` +
                `"${hook.payload.title}"`,
            );
            break;
    }
});

app.listen(5555);
```

Switching on `hook.trigger` after `isKnownWebhook` narrows `hook.payload` to the type that event carries, with no cast.

## Documentation for Verification

Every delivery carries `x-docspace-signature-256`, an HMAC-SHA256 of the raw body keyed with the subscription's secret, formatted as `sha256=` followed by uppercase hexadecimal.

`verifySignature` handles the three details that are easy to get wrong: it hashes the raw body, compares in constant time, and folds case — DocSpace emits uppercase hexadecimal where some other services emit lowercase.

Two further headers, `x-docspace-event-id` and `x-docspace-event-timestamp`, repeat `event.id` and `event.createOn` so a stale or already-seen delivery can be dropped without reading the body. They are **not** covered by the signature: reject on them freely, but never accept on them. The values inside the verified body are the authoritative ones.

Deduplicate on `event.id`. It is stable across the server's automatic retries, though a manual retry by an administrator creates a new record and therefore a new id.

## Documentation for Events

`event.trigger` names the event that caused the delivery, for example `file.created`. `GET api/2.0/settings/webhook/triggers` lists every event, the value to subscribe with, and whether your role may subscribe to it.

Files, folders, rooms, agents and forms all deliver the same payload shape; use `fileEntryType` (1 folder, 2 file) to tell them apart. A file's name arrives in `title`, and a field missing from the JSON means empty, `false` or zero — never "unknown".

New events are added over time. `isKnownWebhook` returns false for one this build does not recognise, and the body stays available on `hook.raw`, so an upgrade cannot break your endpoint.

